---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ClassificationTable from '../components/ClassificationTable.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Probability Simulator: WinGo-Style Classifications" description="Preview a transparent 0-9 educational simulation model. Explore number, size, and overlapping color classifications with clearly stated assumptions.">
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Simulator' }]} /></div>
  <header class="page-header container"><p class="eyebrow">The experiment desk</p><h1>Make chance<br /><em>a little more tangible.</em></h1><p>A local model of ten possible digits. See how a number becomes a size and a set of colors, then understand the assumptions behind the experiment.</p></header>
  <div class="container tool-layout"><section class="tool-panel" aria-labelledby="preview-title"><div class="figure-topline"><span class="eyebrow">Model: uniform-digits-v1</span><span class="pill">Static preview</span></div><h2 id="preview-title">A result, explained.</h2><div class="tool-result"><span class="digit green dual">5</span><div><span class="small-label">ILLUSTRATIVE OUTCOME</span><strong>Big · Green + Violet</strong><p>The digit is one outcome. All three labels describe it.</p></div></div><div class="model-stats"><div><strong>10%</strong><span>one exact digit</span></div><div><strong>50%</strong><span>Big</span></div><div><strong>20%</strong><span>Violet</span></div></div><div class="coming-soon"><strong>Interactive experiment coming soon</strong><p>This is a static teaching example. No timer is running and no new outcomes are generated on this page yet.</p></div></section><aside class="tool-sidebar"><span class="eyebrow">Before the first draw</span><h2>Know your model.</h2><ol class="numbered-list"><li><strong>Ten equally likely digits</strong><span>0 through 9, with a 10% chance for each.</span></li><li><strong>Independent draws</strong><span>A previous outcome does not change the next draw’s probability.</span></li><li><strong>Overlapping colors</strong><span>0 is Red + Violet. 5 is Green + Violet.</span></li></ol><a class="text-link" href="/number-probability/">Understand the probability <Icon /></a></aside></div>
  <section class="container section tool-explanation"><div class="prose"><h2>A local educational experiment</h2><p>The planned simulation generates digits in your browser. It will offer timed rounds and batch samples, record a limited recent history, and compare observed frequencies with theoretical probabilities. The duration of a teaching round changes the pace, not the mathematical model.</p><p>Outcomes will not come from an external game, and the simulation will not forecast one. Repeatable recorded runs will be labeled as replays, rather than fresh random draws.</p><h2>Reading the classifications</h2><p>The table is the selected example ruleset for this site. A number belongs to exactly one size group, but 0 and 5 belong to two color groups. Other versions may use different rules.</p><ClassificationTable /><h2>Observed is different from expected</h2><p>In 20 draws, the expected count of a particular digit is 2. That does not require it to appear exactly twice. Random samples can be uneven; the <a href="/historical-analysis/">historical-analysis guide</a> explains how to read that variation.</p><div class="takeaway"><p>Local educational simulation only. No live results, external predictions, accounts, or financial transactions.</p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
